    id: 'python-3', category: 'Python',
    question: 'How do you design a testing strategy for a Python tool that manages production infrastructure state?',
    followUp: 'What is the difference between mocking the subprocess/API boundary and mocking your own abstraction on top of it, and why does that distinction matter for test value?',
    framework: 'Separate pure logic from side-effecting boundaries -> mock at the narrowest real boundary -> layer unit, integration, and a small number of high-value end-to-end checks -> treat destructive-path tests as first-class.',
    answerStructure: [
      {label: 'Reasoning path', content: 'The core design move is architectural before it is a testing choice: separate the code that decides what to do (pure functions — given this cluster state, compute this diff) from the code that does it (subprocess calls, API clients, file writes), because the decision logic is cheap and fast to unit-test exhaustively while the side-effecting boundary is expensive and risky to exercise for real on every test run. I would design the tool so the side-effecting layer is a thin, narrow interface that can be mocked at exactly that boundary.'},
      {label: 'Mechanism / commands', content: 'Unit tests mock at the narrowest real boundary — the `subprocess.run` call itself, or the HTTP client\'s `.request()` method — using `unittest.mock.patch` with recorded realistic outputs (including malformed and error cases), not a mock of your own wrapper function, which would only test that your mock does what you told it to do. Integration tests run against a real but disposable target (a kind/minikube cluster, a test Slurm instance, a staging BCM head node) to catch the class of bug — schema drift, an undocumented API behavior change — that a mocked unit test structurally cannot.'},
      {label: 'Trade-offs', content: 'Mocking closer to the real boundary (the subprocess call) gives higher-fidelity tests but more brittle setup (you have to keep sample outputs current as the underlying tool\'s output format evolves); mocking your own higher-level wrapper is easier to write but risks becoming a test of your mocks rather than your logic. I would spend the integration-test budget specifically on destructive operations (drain, delete, replace) since those are the ones where a bug in production is expensive, even though they are also the hardest and slowest to test realistically.'},
      {label: 'Common pitfalls', content: 'The most common failure is a test suite with high coverage numbers that mocks at the wrong layer — patching the wrapper function itself so the test asserts "my function calls my function" and would pass even if the underlying kubectl/Terraform invocation were completely wrong. A second common gap is never testing the timeout, malformed-output, and partial-failure paths at all, because they are inconvenient to construct, even though those are exactly the paths that fail silently in production and cause the worst incidents.'},
      {label: 'Interview-ready line', content: '"I mock at the actual process/API boundary with realistic recorded outputs, including malformed and timeout cases, and I reserve a small number of real integration tests against a disposable cluster specifically for destructive operations — a test suite that only mocks its own abstractions can have perfect coverage and still ship a tool that has never actually exercised its failure paths."'},
    ],
