    id: 'k8s-2', category: 'Kubernetes',
    question: 'How does the Kubernetes scheduler actually decide where a Pod runs, and how would you debug a GPU workload stuck Pending with plenty of idle GPU capacity visible in the cluster?',
    followUp: 'What is the difference between a predicate/filter failure and a scoring outcome, and which one explains a Pod that never gets placed at all versus one that lands somewhere unexpected?',
    framework: 'Filter (hard constraints) then score (soft preferences) -> map GPU-specific constraints into that model -> read scheduler events as the primary evidence, not inference.',
    answerStructure: [
      {label: 'Reasoning path', content: 'The scheduler runs two distinct phases per Pod: filtering, which removes any node that cannot possibly run the Pod (insufficient resources, taints without matching tolerations, node affinity mismatch, port conflicts), and scoring, which ranks the survivors by soft preferences (spreading, affinity weights, resource balance) to pick one. A Pod stuck Pending with visible idle capacity means every node failed the filter phase — scoring never even ran — so the investigation starts at "why did every candidate node get filtered out," not at resource totals.'},
      {label: 'Mechanism / commands', content: '`kubectl describe pod <name>` prints the scheduler\'s FailedScheduling events with the specific predicate that rejected each node class (e.g., "0/12 nodes are available: 8 Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu, 4 node(s) had taint {nvidia.com/gpu: NoSchedule} that the pod did not tolerate"). For GPU specifically, the resource `nvidia.com/gpu` only exists as allocatable if the device plugin has successfully advertised it, so idle GPUs on a node whose device plugin crashed or restarted mid-advertisement are invisible to the scheduler even though `nvidia-smi` on that node shows them fine.'},
      {label: 'Trade-offs', content: 'A strong candidate distinguishes "the scheduler is broken" from "the scheduler is correctly enforcing a constraint I forgot I set" — most Pending-with-idle-capacity incidents are the latter (a missing toleration, a node selector that no longer matches after a label change) rather than a scheduler bug, and starting from that prior saves time. Extended resources like `nvidia.com/gpu` are also non-fractional by default (MIG aside), so a request for a fractional GPU with no MIG/time-slicing configuration will filter out every node regardless of idle capacity, which looks identical to a capacity problem in the event message\'s "Insufficient" wording until you read the requested quantity.'},
      {label: 'Common pitfalls', content: 'A weak answer treats "Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu" literally as "no GPUs are free" without checking whether the device plugin is actually advertising allocatable GPUs on those nodes at all — a device-plugin crash silently zeroes allocatable GPU capacity cluster-wide without marking the node NotReady, so `kubectl describe node` and the device plugin\'s own pod status are required evidence, not optional. Another common miss is not checking taints at all, since a GPU node pool is very commonly tainted specifically to keep non-GPU workloads off it, and a missing toleration produces the exact same Pending symptom as a real capacity shortage.'},
      {label: 'Interview-ready line', content: '"Pending with idle capacity almost always means every node failed the filter phase, not that scoring picked badly — I read the FailedScheduling event\'s exact predicate first, and for GPU workloads specifically I check whether the device plugin is actually advertising `nvidia.com/gpu` as allocatable before I trust that visible idle hardware means schedulable capacity."'},
    ],
